            bool lastIndexAssertion = (New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount);

	    // a precondition using "System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(!@@@\(arrList\.Contains\(x\)\);" @@@ array does not contain x post-add
            bool containsAssertion = !arrList.Contains(x);

    }
}
}
```
A contract tests the Add method by assuming an existing list and an item x. It captures the preconditions (old states), the method call (addition of x), and the postconditions (new states). It then uses these values to deduce a precondition that the returned value must be the list size (index of the added element). If the contract holds, the test passes; if not, the contract is reported as failing. The failing contract indicates the failed precondition; in this case, the array does not contain x post-add, hence the contract is violated.

The contract uses the debug assert function to make assertions about the expected behavior of the method, which is the last insert position for the element x in the new array having a greater or equal size as the old array size, and that the array does not contain x after the addition.

Please note, for this code to work, you should have the necessary Pex packages and settings installed in your project. Make sure you have Pex settings enabled as well as the Pex assemblies in your path.

The purpose behind the contracts is to provide a rigorous unit testing methodology for your methods, with the ability to quickly identify and report potential weak points in your tests.

In the same way, if you want to test other methods in the ArrayList class (like Remove), you can expand the test by creating different random inputs for the methods and checking the return values against the expectations.

The NUnit framework should be installed in your project. To write tests using NUnit, you need to install the NUnit Test Adapter and the NUnit 3 Test Project Templates from the Visual Studio Marketplace. In most of the test frameworks, it might take a lot of setup to get them working on code analysis, but once everything is properly configured, the tests will give you instant feedback during your testing. 

Please remember that every test should be designed to test a small unit of work in isolation from the rest of your system. It is easier to test small units of code, and that is the principal goal of unit testing. -->

<!-- Testing principles:
1. Unit testing: It focuses on the smallest possible units of work, in this case, methods that can handle independent execution.
2. Isolated testing: Each test should run in isolation and should not rely on the state of the system or other tests.
3. Repeatable testing: Tests should always produce the same results given the same inputs, making them repeatable.
4. Self-testing: Assertions or if statements are used to verify the expected behaviour and are embedded in your code.

This style of testing is commonly achieved in a programming environment. It is well suited to be run automatically using Continuous Integration (CI) servers and can help to maintain a high-quality codebase by catching issues early and ensuring reliable releases.

In terms of tooling, the most commonly used testing frameworks are NUnit, XUnit, and MSTest. They are all compatible with popular CI/CD tools like Jenkins or Travis CI. And for .NET there is a built-in testing tool, NUnit 3, which is simpler to use than the previous NUnit version.-->

<!-- ## Unit Testing

The purpose of unit testing is to isolate each unit of code, as much as possible, and to verify that it behaves correctly in isolation. 

**Testing is code**: Just as good code relies on testing code, your testing code should be just as thorough. If your tests do not cover the whole project, you didn’t do unit testing hard enough.

**Test should be independent**: Tests should be able to run in any order. That means they should not rely on other tests to run correctly. 

**Correctness versus efficiency**: The goal is not just to have the right answers, but also to get the answers right in the first place. So, keep efficiency in mind during unit testing.

**Good Tests Have Failing Results, Bad Tests Have Passing Results**: Tests should always fail if the code doesn’t meet the test criteria. This way, if the code does not pass its test, you know a problem occurred.

**Test Early and Often**: Test as early as you can in the development process. Not enough testing can cause your program to become complex--------------------        }
        // rest of the contract test file...
    }
}
